 CBS  on May 4th 1970 he was a Kent State  senior also shooting pictures Philo  wanted to document the essence of the  student protests but instead found  himself in the line of fire by the time  I got the camera back around to my eye  it was over the shooting was over I  never hit the ground and never just  stood there like an flat-footed idiot in  shock in shock yeah looking and then you  see this body behind you down on the  pavement and it's the blood is the  volume of blood is enormous it was  something Philo knew had to be seen to  be believed  so we took pictures and then could see  in the one frame the girls running up  and then she kneels down beside the body  and I said I gotta get her I gotta get  him it was almost in profile let me get  it more head-on and then she just let  out with a scream and you shot this  photograph of Mary Vecchio kneeling over  Geoffrey Miller one of four students  killed that day  would win Philo a Pulitzer Prize what  did you think when you saw the image  that we know because no I knew I  actually took the picture it wasn't just  in your mind that I took the picture I  had that moment of doubt because it was  such a shocking the adrenaline rush of  being shot at and surviving Philo's  picture would also inspire a protesting  Ohio performed by crosby Stills Nash and  young  [Music]  after the Kent State shootings more than  four million students protested across  the United States  I think it's remembered in large part  because so many students felt that it  could have been them thomas grace was  one of nine wounded that day i was hit  in the left heel and it just took the  right side of my left foot off now a  history professor grace wrote kent state  death and dissent in the long 60s which  looked at what happened and why he and  others maintained that an audio  recording reanalyzed in 2007 contains  evidence of an order to fire eight  guardsmen were indicted in 1973 for  their involvement but the case was  thrown out the following year grace is  among a group that believes the federal  investigation into the shootings should  be reopened even fifty years later so it  helps us to understand better what took  place there and that there was some  degree of foresight on the part of the  National Guard in terms of what they did  the National Guard always maintained  that they fired in defense of their  lives civil discourse and continued  dialogue surrounding the Kent State  shootings is in part what has motivated  the annual commemorations of that day  including this year's virtual ceremony  capping off a year-long observance of  the half-century gone by and while may  4th 1970 has personal meaning for those  who were there like John Cleary they are  the first to remind us it's a day we all  can and should learn from I know I've  had people and they said you mean there  were military on campus and they  actually shot people and they're what is  the danger of people not knowing what  happened that day the danger is that  it's forgotten  and that it could conceivably happen  again I think to remember and honor the  four students that were slain give some  weight and hopefully prohibits that kind  of action in the future  that commemoration is so important to  those who were there you have to  remember it took almost 20 years for the  university to actually acknowledge that  day they do have a beautiful memorial  that is there now and Jeff the words  that are on part of it enquire learn and  reflect again are so important to all  the men that we spoke with sounds like  good advice just horrifying still to see  those pictures and also to hear John so  vividly talk about what happened back  then Dana thank you very much details  you know
 

